.Dd November 23, 2026
.Dt NVFATBIN 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvfatbin
.Nd the cuda-native fatbin container
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The cuda-native fatbin container holds the device images clang collects for
one CUDA compilation.
It is LLVM's
.Em OffloadBinary
format, version 2: the layout documented in LLVM's
.Pa llvm/Object/OffloadBinary.h .
No NVIDIA fatbin, cubin or ELF format is read, parsed or imitated.
.Pp
The container is a header, one entry per member, one string table per entry
and the member images themselves.
Every field is little-endian and every offset is an absolute byte offset from
the start of the container.
The header, the entries table, each entry's string table and each image start
at an 8-byte aligned offset.
The keys and the values inside the string blob are packed one after another
and are not aligned: their offsets are exact byte offsets, and a reader must
use them as they are.
.Pp
The header is 32 bytes long:
.Bl -tag -width "entries_offset" -compact
.It Dv offset
.It Dv size
.It Dv field
.It 0
.It 4
.It Va magic
the four bytes
.Ql 10 FF 10 AD .
.It 4
.It 4
.It Va version
the container version, 2 .
.It 8
.It 8
.It Va size
the length of the whole container in bytes, including the padding at its end.
.It 16
.It 8
.It Va entries_offset
the offset of the first entry, always 32 .
.It 24
.It 8
.It Va entries_count
the number of entries.
.El
.Pp
Each entry is 40 bytes long:
.Bl -tag -width "num_strings" -compact
.It Dv offset
.It Dv size
.It Dv field
.It 0
.It 2
.It Va image_kind
5 for PTX, 3 for a cubin and 0 for none .
.It 2
.It 2
.It Va offload_kind
2 for CUDA and 0 for none .
.It 4
.It 4
.It Va flags
0 .
.It 8
.It 8
.It Va string_offset
the offset of the entry's string table.
.It 16
.It 8
.It Va num_strings
the number of strings in the table.
.It 24
.It 8
.It Va image_offset
the offset of the member's image.
.It 32
.It 8
.It Va image_size
the length of the member's image in bytes.
.El
.Pp
A string table holds
.Va num_strings
records of 24 bytes each:
.Bl -tag -width "value_size" -compact
.It Dv offset
.It Dv size
.It Dv field
.It 0
.It 8
.It Va key_offset
the offset of the NUL-terminated key.
.It 8
.It 8
.It Va value_offset
the offset of the value.
.It 16
.It 8
.It Va value_size
the length of the value in bytes.
.El
.Pp
A key is a NUL-terminated string.
A value is a byte range; the byte after it is normally a NUL.
.Pp
A value of a key the reader knows must fit the field it belongs to:
.Dv triple
is at most 63 bytes,
.Dv arch
and
.Dv cloudbsd.format
are at most 31 bytes each, and
.Dv cloudbsd.sha256
is exactly 64 hexadecimal digits.
A value that does not fit is refused with its own error, and it is never
truncated.
.Pp
A key the reader does not know is ignored, and its value may be as long as
it likes, so an additive key from another writer does not change the
container.
.Pp
Every member carries these strings:
.Bl -tag -width "cloudbsd.format" -compact
.It Dv triple
.Pa nvptx64-nvidia-cuda .
.It Dv arch
the architecture, for example
.Pa sm_120
or
.Pa sm_121a .
.It Dv cloudbsd.format
.Pa ptx
for a PTX member and
.Pa nvcubin/1
for a cubin member.
.It Dv cloudbsd.sha256
the hexadecimal SHA-256 of the member's image.
.El
.Pp
A container written by LLVM's own
.Xr clang-offload-packager 1
has no
.Dv cloudbsd.*
keys.
A reader accepts such a container and reports those fields as absent.
.Pp
An image kind of
.Dv none
and an offload kind of
.Dv none
mean that the writer did not name the kind of the image or the producer of
the member.
LLVM's own packager writes them, so a reader accepts them; every other kind is
refused.
.Sh VERSIONING
The container version stays 2 as long as the layout stays the same.
New keys may be added: a reader ignores a key it does not know, so an
additive key does not change the version.
A change to the layout itself is a new
.Dv cloudbsd.format
value, not a new version number.
.Sh CHECKS
A reader must refuse a container, with a distinct error for each case, when
any of the following holds:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
the input is shorter than the header;
.It
the magic is not
.Ql 10 FF 10 AD ;
.It
the version is not one it knows;
.It
the size in the header is larger than the input it was given;
.It
the entries table lies outside the container, or the entry count is above
4096;
.It
an image offset and size overflow, or leave the container;
.It
a string offset leaves the container, or the value of a key the reader
knows is not NUL terminated;
.It
a value of a key the reader knows is longer than the field it belongs to;
.It
an image kind is neither PTX, nor a cubin, nor none;
.It
an offload kind is neither CUDA nor none;
.It
a
.Dv cloudbsd.sha256
is not 64 hexadecimal digits, or does not match the image;
.It
the sum of the sizes of the images whose members carry a digest is larger
than the container itself.
.El
.Pp
The sum of the sizes of the images a container hashes may not be larger than
the container itself.
Only the images that are hashed are counted: a member without a
.Dv cloudbsd.sha256
key is not hashed and asks for no work.
Two members that name the same image range and both carry a digest ask for
twice the hashing work one image is worth, so a container that asks for more
work than its size allows is refused before any digest is computed.
.Pp
A reader checks a value of a key it knows once, and it looks at no more of
the value than could fit the field the key belongs to.
A value that does not fit is refused by its length.
The value of a key the reader does not know is ignored, and is never looked
at, so a container cannot ask for the same long value to be checked again
and again.
.Pp
The writer refuses to write a container that a reader or the runtime would
refuse: a container with no members, one with more than 4096 members and
one larger than 16 MiB are refused, each with its own error.
.Pp
A reader must never read past the end of the input it was given, and must
check every offset and length for overflow before it adds them.
.Pp
A reader that is given a pointer without a length cannot take the length in
the header for a bound: the bytes the pointer names may be shorter than the
container claims.
Such a reader bounds every read by the object that holds the pointer.
It finds that object with
.Xr dl_iterate_phdr 3 :
the
.Va PT_LOAD
segment that contains the pointer ends where the readable bytes of that
object end, and no read may pass that end.
The bound is never more than 16 MiB.
A pointer that is in no loaded object is refused.
A program that holds a container in a mapping of its own, which is in no
loaded object, registers that mapping with the reader, and the bound is then
the end of the registered mapping.
A registration never widens the bound of a pointer that a loaded object
already bounds: the loaded object is the authority, and a registration only
answers for a pointer that is in no loaded object at all.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cuda-objdump 1 ,
.Xr nvptxas 1 ,
.Xr libcudart-stub 3 ,
.Xr cuda-native-toolchain 7
.Sh CAVEATS
The container is a cuda-native format built on LLVM's layout.
It is not an NVIDIA fatbinary and no NVIDIA fatbinary tool reads it.
The container carries device code; it neither assembles PTX nor makes device
code executable.
